[Music]  identity and access management or iam in  google cloud gives you fine-grained  control over the permissions that users  have today we're going to look at group  membership and the resource hierarchy to  understand how you can allow users to  access resources  let's start on the iam page where we can  see the permissions for different users  on a project in our project the user  user at tsc.junix.com  has the storage object viewer role this  role allows a user to list and download  objects from storage buckets but does  not allow them to create or delete any  objects this role is granted to the user  in the project's iam policy so it will  apply to the whole project in all  buckets in the project  let's take a look at those buckets by  going to the cloud storage page we'll  also open up the cloud shell to run  commands as this user  by running gsutil ls we can list the  items in the specified bucket  when we try to delete an object by using  gsutil rm the command fails with an  error message because we don't have the  storage.objects.delete permission being  able to list the objects but not delete  them is consistent with having the  storage object viewer roll  [Music]  let's take a look at the other bucket in  this project my underscore bucket  underscore 321  we can list the objects in this bucket  in the same way however when we try to  delete an object we can see that the  command is actually successful  what would cause this difference in  behavior between the two buckets since  both buckets belong to the same project  our user should have the same view only  permissions and shouldn't have been able  to delete that object  to investigate this kind of issue let's  use the iam policy troubleshooter here  you can see more details on why a user  or service account does or does not have  a permission we can put in our user's  email the full resource path to the  bucket and the  storage.objects.delete permission we  want to investigate  to see what format to use for full  resource names check the documentation  the policy troubleshooter shows us that  the user does indeed have permissions to  delete objects rather than having that  permission at the project level the  permission is actually at the bucket  level from the storage object admin role  the permissions from this role allow  users to do things like create and  delete objects in addition to viewing  them having this permission at the  bucket level lets this user delete  objects for that specific bucket even  though they can't delete objects for  other buckets in the same project  we can check the binding details to see  that the reason our user has access is  because they belong to a group that has  the storage object admin role on the  bucket  when using the policy troubleshooter you  may need additional permissions to be  able to see the members of a group  you can read more about the policy  troubleshooter in the documentation and  try it out for yourself in the google  cloud console  [Music]

